{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310828,"nonce":"TkTfxKvG80gdsvZ1w8XFvBMVIr4=","title":"Designed (not yet implemented) upstream Cranelift fix — a vreduce_iadd CLIF op lowering to AArch64 addv — to close rayzor's native SIMD ...","message":"**A/B VERDICT 2026-06-23: a nue-decode A/B CANNOT measure this addv — two independent reasons. (1) STRUCTURAL: nue's native quant-dot hot path is Rust FFI (matmulXTQ / nue-plugins kernels, compiled by rustc+LLVM), NOT rayzor-cranelift VectorReduce. The addv ISLE rule only changes rayzor-cranelift's lowering of pure-Haxe `SIMD4i32.sum()` (the only native source of the `extractlane(iadd_pairwise tree)` pattern — there's no cranelift autovectorizer on rayzor's MIR→CLIF path). nue native decode never routes through it ⇒ expected delta ≈ 0. (2) BLOCKING: nue does NOT run end-to-end native in the current tree anyway — SIGILLs (exit 132) in `[load] reading GGUF` on BOTH `--no-cache` and scrubbed-cache, the documented [[bugs_import_xmodule_member_resolution]] cross-module bug (imported `GGUFLoader.loadWithTokenizer`'s call to `haxe.io.Bytes.alloc` mis-lowers to a bare unresolvable external `alloc`). My addv/VectorReduce change is PROVEN innocent (zero simd/vector/reduce in any failure; failing symbol is `Bytes.alloc`). GOTCHA found+fixed: `target/release/libnue_plugins.dylib` was MISSING (wiped in build churn) → plugin symbol-map unloaded → `KvCacheQ8.alloc` static-extern emitted as bare `alloc`; rebuilt via `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo build -p nue-plugins --release`. NET: the addv's value is the disasm-proven 7→2-instr hsum fold (latent — tightens the native FLOOR for FUTURE self-hosted pure-Haxe kernels; today's FFI decode sees nothing). The local cranelift dep (file:///…/wasmtime branch addv-rayzor-base) is cranelift-jit-IDENTICAL to git-pin 8cb28bc (only +9 lines in aarch64 lower.isle) ⇒ ZERO effect on wasm codegen; safe to leave in place for wasm work. Backups: /tmp/rayzor_Cargo.toml.bak + /tmp/rayzor_compiler_Cargo.toml.bak (original github git-pin lines).**\n\n**STATUS 2026-06-23: peephole version SHIPPED as a PR — [bytecodealliance/wasmtime#13717](https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/13717) (OPEN), branch darmie:aarch64-addv-hsum off upstream/main 160f104.** Landed the SMALL AArch64-only peephole first (darmie's choice, modeled on his merged SDOT PR #13640); the full vreduce_iadd op below is proposed in the PR body as a follow-up. The peephole: ONE rule in cranelift/codegen/src/isa/aarch64/lower.isle (extractlane section) — `(rule 3 (lower (extractlane $I32 (iadd_pairwise $I32X4 (iadd_pairwise $I32X4 x x) (iadd_pairwise $I32X4 x x)) (u8_from_uimm8 0))) (mov_from_vec (addv x (vector_size $I32X4)) 0 (scalar_size $I32)))` — folds the i32x4 hsum tree (`addp; addp; umov`, 3 instrs) to `addv; umov` (2 instrs, no serial chain). Verified via clif-util: emits addv; 2 new filetests (isa/aarch64/simd-addv-reduce.clif precise-output + runtests/simd-addv-reduce.clif interpret+run); full aarch64 isa (116) + runtests (384) green. GVN shares the inner iadd_pairwise; the egraph has NO iadd_pairwise rules so the structured tree survives to isel (this robustness is WHY the peephole works where a plain iadd tree wouldn't). LOCAL-BUILD GOTCHA: upstream/main (newer than the SDOT base) + my nightly (1.96.0 2026-04-07) hit a corrupt-incremental-cache ICE (on_disk_cache.rs:663) after `git reset --hard` churn — fixed with CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 (CI uses the right toolchain, so the PR builds fine). **RAYZOR-SIDE FOLLOW-UP (after #13717 merges):** change cranelift_backend.rs VectorReduce{Add,!float} arm (~4631) to emit the iadd_pairwise reduction (currently an extract+iadd tree) so it matches the new rule, then bump the cranelift rev in root Cargo.toml (~97-103) + compiler/Cargo.toml (~64-65) to the merge commit. NOTE: even WITHOUT the upstream PR, emitting iadd_pairwise alone gets 7→3 instrs; the PR is the marginal 3→2.\n\n**Designed 2026-06-23 (4-agent workflow, disassembly-verified) — this vreduce_iadd op is the PROPOSED FOLLOW-UP, NOT yet implemented:** Goal: rayzor's native (Cranelift) SIMD integer lane-sum (`SIMD4i32.sum()` / i32x4 quant-accumulator hsum, MIR `IrInstruction::VectorReduce{op:Add}` at cranelift_backend.rs:4631) currently lowers to an **extract+add tree = 7 AArch64 instrs, no `addv`** (verified via clif-util: `mov w_,v0.s[0..3]` ×4 + 3 serial `add`s). AArch64 `addv` exists in Cranelift (used internally for vhigh_bits) but **no CLIF op targets it** — CLIF has no horizontal-add reduce (only vhigh_bits/vall_true/vany_true/swizzle). `ireduce` is integer WIDTH-narrowing, unrelated.\n\n**RECOMMENDED FIX = new CLIF op `vreduce_iadd`** (integer horizontal add), NOT an ISLE peephole. Peephole rejected: cranelift/codegen/src/opts/cprop.isle reassociates commutative trees + egraph GVN normalizes extractlane+iadd before isel → a tree-matcher wouldn't fire reliably; and rayzor already emits a SINGLE VectorReduce op (builds the tree by hand) so there's nothing to pattern-match — it can just emit one new op. Same strategy as darmie's merged SDOT PR #13640. Verified win = **7→2 instrs** (`addv s_, v_.4s` + `umov w0, v_.s[0]` — the umov is unavoidable to get the scalar into a GPR), and it kills the 3-deep serial add dependency chain. Scope = INTEGER lanes only (float hsum stays a tree; non-assoc-FP complicates the verifier spec; the hot kernel accumulator is i32x4 anyway). VectorDot→SDOT is already optimal, out of scope.\n\n**EXACT upstream changes (wasmtime monorepo /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/wasmtime; sibling at SDOT-PR HEAD 532bec1bfa, ahead of rayzor's pin 8cb28bc):**\n1. Meta op def: cranelift/codegen/meta/src/shared/instructions.rs after the vhigh_bits push (~1652). `Inst::new(\"vreduce_iadd\", ..., \u0026formats.unary).operands_in(vec![Operand::new(\"a\", IxN)]).operands_out(vec![Operand::new(\"a\", \u0026IxN.lane_of())])` — controlling typevar = input vector IxN, output = IxN.lane_of() (passes is_ctrl_typevar_candidate like vhigh_bits).\n2. AArch64 lower: cranelift/codegen/src/isa/aarch64/lower.isle after vhigh_bits rules (~2924). For lane_fits_in_32: `(rule 1 (lower (vreduce_iadd _ vec @ (value_type (lane_fits_in_32 in_ty)))) (mov_from_vec (addv vec (vector_size in_ty)) 0 (scalar_size (lane_type in_ty))))`. For I64X2 (addv has no 2D mode): `(rule (lower (vreduce_iadd _ vec @ (value_type $I64X2))) (mov_from_vec (addp vec vec (VectorSize.Size64x2)) 0 (ScalarSize.Size64)))`. Helpers addv/addp/mov_from_vec/vector_size/scalar_size/lane_fits_in_32 all already exist.\n3. CROSS-ISA fallback (REQUIRED for upstream acceptance — no auto-legalizer; each ISA lowers vhigh_bits by hand): x64 lower.isle uses x64_phaddd (i32x4) / x64_phaddw (i16x8) + a generic extract+iadd helper for i8x16/i64x2; s390x/pulley/riscv64 (has_v has a native vector reduce) need rules too. Simplest acceptable: one shared generic extract+iadd decomposition for the non-AArch64 ISAs.\n4. Interpreter (cranelift/codegen/src/.../interpreter) + verifier + filetests: cranelift/filetests/filetests/runtests/simd-vreduce-iadd.clif modeled on simd-vhighbits.clif (`test interpret`/`test run`, all targets, `; run: %f([1 2 3 4]) == 10`).\n\n**Rayzor-side (after PR lands):** cranelift_backend.rs VectorReduce arm (4631-4672) — for `op==Add \u0026\u0026 !is_float` emit `builder.ins().vreduce_iadd(vec_val)`, else keep the extract-tree. Do NOT touch wasm_backend.rs (wasm has no addv) or the VectorDot/SDOT arm. Then bump the cranelift rev in BOTH root Cargo.toml (~97-103, all 7 cranelift-* crates) AND compiler/Cargo.toml (~64-65 wasmtime+wasi-common) to the SAME merge commit (resolver drops the JIT if they diverge), `cargo update -p cranelift-codegen`, rebuild `-p rayzor --bin rayzor`.\n\n**PR-ability:** acceptable to bytecodealliance/wasmtime IF cross-ISA + interpreter + runtests are included (reviewers will require all that, not AArch64-only). Effort ~1-2 days. Full design in workflow wf_b373a125-252 transcript. Caveat: confirm the hsum is hot enough to matter (per-token vs per-matmul-tile) before investing — the perf memories say native is already ~llama.cpp-CPU parity; this mainly tightens the native floor + is a clean upstream contribution.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_cranelift_addv_upstream` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}